# Credentials Wrangler reads when it deploys. Copy this file to `.env` and fill
# it in. `.env` is gitignored, and the commit guard blocks it by path as well,
# because .gitignore does not stop `git add -f`.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Creating the token
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# Make it a USER token: My Profile -> API Tokens -> Create Token. Cloudflare's
# docs say Wrangler supports only user tokens, and that account-owned ones
# (cfat_ prefix, issued from the "Account API Tokens" page) are not supported
# yet.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Read this before debugging an auth failure
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# A real environment variable beats this file. If CLOUDFLARE_API_TOKEN is
# already set in your shell or at user scope, Wrangler uses that one and never
# looks here, and nothing says so. On 2026-08-11 a stale user-scope token cost
# an hour of debugging: Wrangler answered `Invalid access token [code: 9109]`
# and `Authentication error [code: 10000]` while the token in this file worked
# perfectly against the REST API, which looked like proof that Wrangler was
# rejecting the token. It was not. It was reading a different one.
#
# Check before you conclude anything:
#
#   powershell: [Environment]::GetEnvironmentVariable('CLOUDFLARE_API_TOKEN')
#   bash:       printenv CLOUDFLARE_API_TOKEN
#
# Compare the two by HASH, and do not print either value.
#
# Comparing the prefix and the length does not work, and this file used to say to
# do exactly that. Every Cloudflare user token is `cfut_` followed by 48
# characters, so any two valid tokens match on both. On 2026-08-11 a token in the
# environment and a token in .env were identical in shape while one returned
# `Invalid access token [code: 9109]` and the other worked. Length is also a
# disclosure: it narrows a guess, and terminals end up in screenshots.
#
#   $sha = [Security.Cryptography.SHA256]::Create()
#   ([BitConverter]::ToString($sha.ComputeHash(
#     [Text.Encoding]::UTF8.GetBytes($value))) -replace '-','').Substring(0,12)
#
# `npx baseclf secret set` and `npx create-baseclf` both compare by value and warn
# when the environment is shadowing this file, so neither needs you to do this by
# hand. It is here for the times you are debugging wrangler directly.
#
# Also note that `Authentication error [code: 10000]` arrives with a hint
# recommending you check the token's permissions. That hint sends you to fix
# something that may be entirely correct.
#
# Choose "Create Custom Token", not a template. The "Edit Cloudflare Workers"
# template looks like the right one and does not include D1, so provisioning
# fails with a 403 that does not mention the missing permission.
#
# Account scope. Six permissions, and no more than six:
#
#   Workers Scripts     Edit    deploy the Worker
#   D1                  Edit    create the database and run its migrations
#   Workers R2 Storage  Edit    create the bucket that uploads go into
#   Account Settings    Read    let Wrangler resolve which account to use
#   Account Analytics   Read    read cpuTimeP50 and cpuTimeP99 from GraphQL
#   Workers Tail        Read    `wrangler tail`, for live logs and exceptions
#
# KV is deliberately absent, and it is absent because nothing reads it. A KV
# namespace was provisioned for a while and no code ever touched it: JWKS caching
# uses the Cache API instead, because KV has a sixty second minimum TTL. A
# resource that exists and means nothing never breaks, which is why it survived
# as long as it did.
#
# R2 used to be absent from this list too, and that was already wrong by the time
# anybody read it: the Worker has required an R2 binding since storage shipped,
# so a token built from the old list could deploy and then fail to provision.
#
# Two things the permissions page does to trip people up. Every permission is
# listed twice under different names, Read/Edit for the dashboard and
# Read/Write for the API, and picking the wrong pair produces a 403 that reads
# like the token is invalid. And "Account Resources" defaults to all accounts;
# narrow it to the one account this deployment lives in.
#
# Set an expiry. This token can deploy code and read a database, so it should
# not outlive the work it was made for.

CLOUDFLARE_API_TOKEN=

# Optional. Wrangler usually infers it when the token is scoped to one account.
# Set it only if a command says it cannot tell which account to use.
CLOUDFLARE_ACCOUNT_ID=
